<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Mahameghavahana dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 100 CE (approx.) · capital Kalinganagari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Kalinga dynasty of the warrior king Kharavela, whose Hathigumpha inscription is one of ancient India's great royal records.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the decline of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kalinga">Kalinga</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a> coast regained its independence under kings who claimed descent from the ancient <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chedi">Chedi</a> line. The dynasty is known almost entirely from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathigumpha-inscription">Hathigumpha inscription</a> carved in a cave on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayagiri-and-khandagiri-caves">Udayagiri</a> hill near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhubaneswar">Bhubaneswar</a>, probably in the 1st century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The inscription records year by year the deeds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharavela">Kharavela</a>, a devout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a>: he repaired the capital Kalinganagari after a cyclone, dug canals, staged festivals and led campaigns against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty</a> to the west, against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Magadha</a>, from which he recovered a Jain image carried off by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanda-dynasty">Nanda</a> king, and against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Pandyas</a> in the far south. He and his queen excavated rock-cut monastic cells for Jain monks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayagiri-and-khandagiri-caves">Udayagiri</a> and Khandagiri.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharavela">Kharavela's</a> successors, Kudepasiri and Vadukha, are known only from short inscriptions, and the dynasty fades from the record by the 1st century. Its rock-cut caves remain an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jain</a> site.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mahameghavahana-dynasty.jpg" alt="Mahameghavahana dynasty" width="320" height="296"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan. Reference: · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Maha-Meghavahanas.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 100 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kalinganagari</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kharavela</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chedi dynasty of Kalinga, Kingdom of Kharavela</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-50&amp;focus=mahameghavahana-dynasty" aria-label="Mahameghavahana dynasty on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6837 1350 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mahameghavahana dynasty lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7253 1492h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7157 1606h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7157 1606h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=-50&amp;focus=mahameghavahana-dynasty">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
